<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharmakshema</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">385–433</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dharmakshema was an Indian Buddhist monk and translator who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Northern Liang ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a>. At the Northern Liang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a>, modern Wuwei, he translated many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> scriptures into Chinese, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahaparinirvana-sutra">Mahaparinirvana Sutra</a>, whose teaching that all beings possess Buddha-nature became central to Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He was murdered in 433 on Mengxun's orders, by tradition as he set out for the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dharmakshema.jpg" alt="Dharmakshema" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 〔北涼〕曇無讖譯 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NLC892-ZHCH0138-199384_%E5%A4%A7%E6%96%B9%E7%AD%89%E7%84%A1%E6%83%B3%E7%B6%93_%E7%AC%AC2%E5%86%8A.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
